## What this is for

Every score contains error. A student who scores 27 on a test today might score 25 or 29 tomorrow for reasons that have nothing to do with what the test is meant to measure: a lucky guess, a misread question, a bad night's sleep. [Constructs and construct maps](constructs.qmd) asked what a scale is meant to measure; this lesson asks how precisely it does so. Classical test theory (CTT) is the oldest and still the most widely used framework for thinking about that error. It is a theory of *test scores*, not of item responses: nothing in this lesson refers to the probability of answering any one item correctly. That is both its main convenience and its central limitation. The payoff is **reliability**, a single number that says how much of the variation in scores is signal.

Reliability estimates are everywhere. That doesn't mean they are always meaningful, and part of this lesson is about when they are not.

## Goals

By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

- write down the classical model and define reliability in terms of it;
- explain why the correlation between two parallel forms estimates reliability;
- compute Cronbach's alpha, and say why it is a lower bound on reliability (and when it is exact);
- use split halves and the Spearman–Brown formula;
- run a classical item analysis and use it to spot items that don't belong, including items that need to be reverse-keyed.

## Core ideas

### True scores and error

CTT grew out of Spearman's attempt to correct correlations for the error in the measures being correlated ([Spearman, 1904](https://doi.org/10.2307/1412159)), and its standard statement is Lord and Novick's (1968). The foundational idea is that a respondent's observed score $X$ is the sum of a **true score** $T$ and an error $E$:
$$X = T + E.$$
The true score is defined as the average score the respondent would get over (hypothetical) endless independent replications of the test; the error is whatever is left. From that definition, errors have mean zero and are uncorrelated with true scores, so the variance splits cleanly:
$$\sigma^2_X = \sigma^2_T + \sigma^2_E.$$
**Reliability** is the share of observed-score variance that is true-score variance:
$$\rho_{XX'} = \frac{\sigma^2_T}{\sigma^2_X} = \frac{\sigma^2_T}{\sigma^2_T + \sigma^2_E}.$$
It is also the squared correlation between observed and true scores (Lord & Novick, 1968). The trouble, of course, is that we never observe $T$, so this definition cannot be computed from data. The rest of CTT is a set of tricks for getting around that.

### The first trick: parallel forms

Suppose we have two forms of a test, $X$ and $X'$, that measure the same true score with the same error variance and independent errors. What can we say about the correlation between them? A little algebra (problem 1 asks you to do it) shows that
$$\text{cor}(X, X') = \frac{\sigma^2_T}{\sigma^2_X} = \rho_{XX'}.$$
The correlation between two observable things equals a quantity defined in terms of an unobservable one. Here is that fact in action.

#### Try it: parallel forms

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof pf_se = Inputs.range([0.1, 2.5], {value: 0.7, step: 0.05, label: "Error SD (true-score SD = 1)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
pf = {
  const r = rng(11), n = 400, T = [], X = [], X2 = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const t = r.norm(); T.push(t); X.push(t + pf_se * r.norm()); X2.push(t + pf_se * r.norm()); }
  return {T, X, X2, pts: X.map((x, i) => ({x, x2: X2[i]}))};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: 460, height: 400, aspectRatio: 1,
  x: {label: "Score on form X", domain: [-6, 6]}, y: {label: "Score on form X′", domain: [-6, 6]},
  marks: [Plot.dot(pf.pts, {x: "x", y: "x2", r: 2.5, fill: palette.main, fillOpacity: 0.45}), Plot.linearRegressionY(pf.pts, {x: "x", y: "x2", stroke: palette.contrast})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Reliability from the definition (true-score variance ÷ observed variance): **${(1 / (1 + pf_se ** 2)).toFixed(2)}**.
Correlation between the two forms in this sample of 400: **${cor(pf.X, pf.X2).toFixed(2)}**.
Standard error of measurement (the SD of the errors): **${pf_se.toFixed(2)}**, so a respondent's observed score typically lands within about ±${(2 * pf_se).toFixed(1)} of their true score.`
```

The two numbers at the top track each other as the slider moves, up to sampling error in 400 simulated respondents. The **standard error of measurement** in the last line is what reliability means for an individual. Reliability is a property of scores in a population; the standard error of measurement, $\sigma_E = \sigma_X\sqrt{1 - \rho_{XX'}}$, is the typical distance between one respondent's observed and true scores. A reliability of 0.8, between the two real scales below (0.70 and 0.90), still leaves each score with an error SD of $\sqrt{1 - 0.8} \approx 0.45$ times the SD of the scores, nearly half their spread. Note, too, that CTT gives one standard error of measurement for everyone, whatever their score. We will see in [Information, precision, and short forms](information.qmd) that item response models let it vary.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "Two parallel forms of a reading test correlate 0.81 in a large sample. What is the reliability of *one* form?",
  options: [
    {text: "0.81", correct: true, why: "For parallel forms, the correlation between them *is* the reliability of each. No square or square root needed."},
    {text: "0.90, the square root of 0.81", correct: false, why: "0.90 is the correlation between observed and true scores, $\\sqrt{\\rho_{XX'}}$. Reliability itself is the correlation between parallel forms."},
    {text: "It can't be known without true scores", correct: false, why: "That is the point of the parallel-forms trick: two observable scores stand in for the unobservable true score."}
  ]
})
```

### Alpha: reliability from one administration

Parallel forms are expensive, and test-retest designs have their own problems (respondents remember items, and they change between occasions). The appeal of **Cronbach's alpha** ([Cronbach, 1951](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02310555)) is that it gives a reliability estimate from a single administration, by treating the items themselves as a set of little parallel tests. For a test of $k$ items with item variances $\sigma^2_i$ and total-score variance $\sigma^2_X$,
$$\alpha = \frac{k}{k-1}\left(1 - \frac{\sum_i \sigma^2_i}{\sigma^2_X}\right).$$
When items are scored 0/1, this is the same thing as the Kuder–Richardson formula 20, KR-20 ([Kuder & Richardson, 1937](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02288391)); Guttman's $\lambda_3$ is alpha under another name ([Guttman, 1945](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02288892)). The intuition: if items hang together, the total-score variance is much larger than the sum of the item variances, because the covariances between items add in; if they don't, the two are similar and alpha is small.

Alpha rests on an assumption, though. Which one? Alpha equals reliability when every item measures the true score *equally well* (the items are "tau-equivalent"). When some items are better measures than others, which is to say nearly always, alpha is *smaller* than reliability ([Novick & Lewis, 1967](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289400)).

#### Try it: when does alpha equal reliability?

Six items, each an equally noisy measure of the same true score, but with loadings on it that you can pull apart.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof al_spread = Inputs.range([0, 1.4], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "How unequal the items are"})
viewof al_err = Inputs.range([0.3, 2], {value: 1, step: 0.05, label: "Error SD per item"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
al_calc = (spread, err) => {
  const k = 6, lam = Array.from({length: k}, (_, i) => 1 + spread * (i - (k - 1) / 2) / ((k - 1) / 2));
  const S = lam.reduce((u, v) => u + v, 0), varX = S * S + k * err * err;
  const sumVar = lam.reduce((u, l) => u + l * l + err * err, 0);
  return {lam, rel: S * S / varX, alpha: k / (k - 1) * (1 - sumVar / varX)};
}
al_now = al_calc(al_spread, al_err)
al_curve = grid(0, 1.4, 71).flatMap(s => { const c = al_calc(s, al_err); return [{s, v: c.rel, what: "Reliability"}, {s, v: c.alpha, what: "Alpha"}]; })
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 300, x: {label: "How unequal the items are"}, y: {label: null, domain: [0, 1]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["Reliability", "Alpha"], range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [Plot.line(al_curve, {x: "s", y: "v", stroke: "what", strokeWidth: 2.5}), Plot.ruleX([al_spread], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Item loadings: ${al_now.lam.map(l => l.toFixed(2)).join(", ")}. Reliability **${al_now.rel.toFixed(3)}**; alpha **${al_now.alpha.toFixed(3)}**${al_spread === 0 ? ", identical, because the items are equally good measures." : `, which understates reliability by ${(al_now.rel - al_now.alpha).toFixed(3)}.`}`
```

With the error SD at 1, the gap stays under 0.01 until "How unequal" passes about 0.5, where the loadings already run from 0.8 to 1.2. Against a reliability near 0.86, that is small, which is part of why alpha has survived. Push the loadings much further apart (at the far right one of them turns negative, an item pointing the wrong way) and the gap grows. But it only ever goes one way.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Proof: alpha is a lower bound on reliability

**Assumptions:** the classical model for each item, $X_i = T_i + E_i$, with errors uncorrelated with each other and with every true score. The result is due to [Novick and Lewis (1967)](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289400); Guttman (1945) had shown it for $\lambda_3$.

Write the total score as a sum of items, $X = \sum_{i=1}^k X_i$. The test's true score is $T = \sum_i T_i$, and its reliability is $\rho = \sigma^2_T / \sigma^2_X$.

**Step 1: rewrite alpha in terms of true scores.** Because errors are uncorrelated with everything else,
$$\sigma^2_X = \sigma^2_T + \sum_i \sigma^2_{E_i} \qquad\text{and}\qquad \sigma^2_{X_i} = \sigma^2_{T_i} + \sigma^2_{E_i}.$$
Subtracting, the error variances cancel:
$$\sigma^2_X - \sum_i \sigma^2_{X_i} = \sigma^2_T - \sum_i \sigma^2_{T_i}.$$
So
$$\alpha = \frac{k}{k-1}\cdot\frac{\sigma^2_X - \sum_i\sigma^2_{X_i}}{\sigma^2_X} = \frac{k}{k-1}\cdot\frac{\sigma^2_T - \sum_i\sigma^2_{T_i}}{\sigma^2_X}.$$

**Step 2: compare with reliability.** Both $\alpha$ and $\rho$ have $\sigma^2_X$ in the denominator, so $\alpha \le \rho$ exactly when
$$\frac{k}{k-1}\left(\sigma^2_T - \sum_i\sigma^2_{T_i}\right) \le \sigma^2_T \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \sigma^2_T \le k\sum_i \sigma^2_{T_i}.$$

**Step 3: that inequality always holds.** Writing $\sigma_i$ for the standard deviation of $T_i$,
$$\sigma^2_T = \sum_i\sum_j \text{Cov}(T_i, T_j) \le \sum_i\sum_j \sigma_i\sigma_j = \Big(\sum_i \sigma_i\Big)^2 \le k\sum_i\sigma_i^2,$$
where the first inequality uses $|\text{Cov}(T_i,T_j)| \le \sigma_i\sigma_j$ and the second is the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality. Hence $\alpha \le \rho$. $\blacksquare$

**When is it exact?** Both inequalities in step 3 are equalities only when every pair of item true scores is perfectly correlated *and* every $\sigma_i$ is the same, that is, when the items' true scores differ by at most a constant ($T_i = T_j + c_{ij}$). This is **essential tau-equivalence**, the case the widget shows at "How unequal = 0". The proof leans on the errors being uncorrelated; if they are correlated (items that share a passage, say), alpha can *overstate* reliability, and the lower-bound guarantee is gone ([Green & Hershberger, 2000](https://doi.org/10.1207/S15328007SEM0702_6)).
:::

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "A colleague reports alpha = 0.72 for a 12-item scale and concludes that 'at least 72% of the score variance is true-score variance.' When is that conclusion safe?",
  options: [
    {text: "Always: alpha is a lower bound", correct: false, why: "Only under the classical assumptions, in particular uncorrelated errors. Correlated errors (items sharing a stem, a passage, a method) can push alpha *above* reliability."},
    {text: "When the item errors are uncorrelated", correct: true, why: "That's the assumption the proof needs. Given it, alpha ≤ reliability, with equality only for (essentially) tau-equivalent items."},
    {text: "Only when the scale is unidimensional", correct: false, why: "Unidimensionality isn't what the proof uses. Alpha can be high for a multidimensional scale; it is not a test of unidimensionality."}
  ]
})
```

### Split halves and the Spearman–Brown formula

An older route to a single-administration estimate is to split the test into two halves, score each, and correlate them. The halves are only half as long as the test, though, and shorter tests are less reliable. The **Spearman–Brown** formula ([Spearman, 1910](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8295.1910.tb00206.x); [Brown, 1910](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8295.1910.tb00207.x)) says how reliability changes when a test is lengthened (or shortened) by a factor $m$ with comparable items:
$$\rho_{m} = \frac{m\,\rho}{1 + (m-1)\rho}.$$
Setting $m = 2$ steps a half-test correlation up to full-test reliability. The same formula answers design questions: how long does a test need to be?

#### Try it: lengthening a test

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof sb_rho = Inputs.range([0.1, 0.95], {value: 0.6, step: 0.01, label: "Reliability of the current test"})
viewof sb_m = Inputs.range([0.25, 5], {value: 2, step: 0.25, label: "Length multiplier m"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
sb = (rho, m) => m * rho / (1 + (m - 1) * rho)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 280, x: {label: "Length multiplier m", domain: [0.25, 5]}, y: {label: "Reliability", domain: [0, 1]},
  marks: [Plot.line(grid(0.25, 5, 96).map(m => ({m, r: sb(sb_rho, m)})), {x: "m", y: "r", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2.5}),
          Plot.dot([{m: sb_m, r: sb(sb_rho, sb_m)}], {x: "m", y: "r", r: 6, fill: palette.contrast}), Plot.ruleY([sb_rho], {stroke: palette.rule})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`A test with reliability ${sb_rho.toFixed(2)}, made ${sb_m}× as long, would have reliability **${sb(sb_rho, sb_m).toFixed(2)}**. Notice the diminishing returns: each extra item buys less than the one before.`
```

Which split should you use? There are a great many ways to split a test in half, and they give different answers. Cronbach (1951) showed that alpha is the *average* split-half coefficient over all possible splits (for the Spearman–Brown version, strictly, when the halves have equal variances). We check this with real data below.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "A 10-item scale has reliability 0.70. Roughly how many comparable items would it need to reach 0.85?",
  options: [
    {text: "About 24", correct: true, why: "Solve $0.85 = 0.70m/(1 + 0.70(m-1))$ for $m$: $m = \\frac{0.85 \\times 0.30}{0.70 \\times 0.15} \\approx 2.4$, so about 24 items."},
    {text: "About 15", correct: false, why: "That assumes reliability rises in proportion to length. Spearman–Brown has diminishing returns: 15 items give only about 0.78."},
    {text: "About 40", correct: false, why: "Forty items (m = 4) would give about 0.90, more than needed."}
  ]
})
```

### Item analysis, and reading your items

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [Item response data and the IRW](irw-data.qmd): IRW tables are *long*, one row per respondent–item response, so an item analysis starts by reshaping to one row per respondent; item means and sum scores are then column and row sums. Item means and item-rest correlations were two of that lesson's four first descriptives.
:::

CTT gives those two first-look numbers their classical roles. For each item we compute:

- its **mean** (for 0/1 items, the proportion correct, often called the item's *p*-value), a first indication of difficulty, where larger values mean easier items;
- its **item-rest correlation**: the correlation between the item and the sum of the *other* items. This tells us whether the item is hanging together with the rest. An item with a correlation near zero, or negative, is either measuring something else or is keyed backwards.

That last point is subtler than it sounds. CTT assumes every item is coded so that a higher response means more of the construct. Many scales deliberately include items worded in the opposite direction, and those have to be **reverse-keyed** before any of this makes sense. How do we know which ones? We will see below that the data take us most of the way but not all of it, and that the last step is to read the items.

## Simulate

Here is the lower-bound result from the proof above, on simulated data where we know the true scores. The code generates a six-item test from the classical model, then compares the true reliability, $\text{cor}(X, T)^2$, with alpha. It is base R only, so it starts quickly.

Run it as is: with equal loadings the two numbers agree up to sampling error (0.865 and 0.86 with the seed as written). Then swap in the line of unequal loadings and run it again. True reliability rises a little, to 0.879, because the steeper items carry more signal, while alpha falls to 0.829: the gap opens, and in the direction the proof says. Change `np` to 100000 and the sampling noise goes away but the gap doesn't.


::: {.cell}
```{webr}
#| results: markup
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| include: true
#| label: unnamed-chunk-15
# Is alpha a lower bound on reliability? Simulate a test from the classical model,
# where we know the true scores, and compare. Change `loadings` and run again.
# Base R only.
set.seed(1)
np <- 1000                               # respondents
loadings <- c(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)          # equal: "tau-equivalent" items
# loadings <- c(0.3, 0.6, 1, 1, 1.5, 2)  # unequal: try this line instead
err_sd <- 1                              # error SD for every item

true <- rnorm(np)
items <- sapply(loadings, function(l) l * true + rnorm(np, sd = err_sd))
X <- rowSums(items)                      # observed sum score
tau <- sum(loadings) * true              # its true score, T

alpha <- function(x) { k <- ncol(x); k / (k - 1) * (1 - sum(apply(x, 2, var)) / var(rowSums(x))) }
cat("true reliability, cor(X, tau)^2:", round(cor(X, tau)^2, 3), "\n")
cat("Cronbach's alpha:             ", round(alpha(items), 3), "\n")

plot(tau, X, pch = 16, col = rgb(0.15, 0.5, 0.89, 0.3),
     xlab = "True score T", ylab = "Observed score X",
     main = paste("reliability", round(cor(X, tau)^2, 2), "| alpha", round(alpha(items), 2)))

```
:::


