## With real data



We use two tables. The main example is [gilbert_meta_1](https://itemresponsewarehouse.org/tables/gilbert_meta_1/), the reading-comprehension outcome of a school-randomized trial of a literacy intervention for third graders ([Gilbert, Kim & Miratrix, 2023](https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986231171710); data at <https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/QARRYT>): 30 items scored 0/1, where 1 is a correct answer. The contrast, for reverse keying, is the Mach IV, a 20-item attitude scale measuring Machiavellianism ([Christie & Geis, 1970](https://doi.org/10.1016/C2013-0-10497-7)), in responses collected by [Hunter, Gerbing and Boster (1982)](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.43.6.1293) and distributed with the lessR R package ([Gerbing, 2026](https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.lessR); IRW table [lessR_Mach4](https://itemresponsewarehouse.org/tables/lessr_mach4/)). All code is base R; [download it](code/ctt-reliability-irw.R).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="show"}
# IRW tables are long (one row per response); reshape to one row per person.
long2wide <- function(df) {
  wide <- tapply(df$resp, list(df$id, df$item), function(x) x[1])
  as.data.frame(wide[, order(colnames(wide))])
}
# Cronbach's alpha (KR-20 when items are 0/1).
alpha <- function(resp) {
  resp <- resp[complete.cases(resp), ]
  k <- ncol(resp)
  (k / (k - 1)) * (1 - sum(apply(resp, 2, var)) / var(rowSums(resp)))
}
# Correlation of each item with the sum of the *other* items.
item_rest <- function(resp) {
  tot <- rowSums(resp)
  sapply(names(resp), function(i) cor(resp[[i]], tot - resp[[i]], use = "complete.obs"))
}
```
:::



